{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"analogical-adroit-urson","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\n`scripts/mutation_gate.py:_diagnose_non_killed` (line 660, called from line 731)\nexecutes mutated shard code through `mutmut run` WITHOUT the OS sandbox added by\n`e668-b496-e264-4283`:\n\n```python\nenv = _command_env(root)          # not mutation_sandbox.sandbox_env(...)\n_run(\n    (sys.executable, \"-m\", \"mutmut\", \"run\", *mutants, \"--max-children\", \"1\"),\n    cwd=root, env=env,            # not mutation_sandbox.wrap(...)\n)\n```\n\nBoth calls in `execute_shard()` are wrapped; this third one is not. It is therefore a\nlive path of exactly the 2026-08-26 incident's shape (`mutmut run` executing mutated\ncode with unrestricted filesystem write access), on `main` today.\n\n## Why this call site is the WORST one to leave unsandboxed\n\n`_diagnose_non_killed` re-runs precisely the mutants that were **not killed** —\nsurvived, timed out, or errored. A mutant that deletes a directory tree and still\nleaves the assertions passing, or that is killed by the 60-second subprocess timeout\nrather than by a test, lands in exactly that set. The 2026-08-26 mutant was stopped by\nthe timeout, so it would have been re-run here. This function selects for destructive\nmutants and then runs them with no sandbox.\n\nIt also skips `sandbox_env()`, so the hardened non-existent `HOME` is absent too.\n\n## Why e668's tests could not catch it\n\n`e668` added AST call-site-binding tests asserting the wrapper is present, but they\ninspect only `execute_shard`. A third `mutmut run` elsewhere in the module is invisible\nto them. `e668`'s plan enumerated the four subprocesses spawned by `execute_shard()`\nand verified that function's two callers, but never asked whether `mutmut run` appears\nelsewhere in the file. It does.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The `mutmut run` in `_diagnose_non_killed` is wrapped in `mutation_sandbox.wrap`\n      with the same allow-list contract as the two calls in `execute_shard`, and uses\n      `mutation_sandbox.sandbox_env`. Verified by a test asserting an unsandboxed write\n      from a mutant re-run under this path is DENIED (enforcement, not argv shape).\n- [ ] The `--max-children 1` concurrency contract on this invocation is preserved.\n- [ ] The AST call-site-binding test is generalised from `execute_shard` to **every**\n      `mutmut run` invocation in the module, so a future fourth call site fails the test\n      rather than silently reintroducing the hole. Verified by removing the wrapper at\n      the `_diagnose_non_killed` site and observing the test go RED.\n- [ ] A test enumerates all `mutmut run` call sites in `mutation_gate.py` and asserts\n      each is sandbox-wrapped, so the control cannot be defeated by adding a call site.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- No `mutmut run` invocation in `mutation_gate.py` executes mutated code unsandboxed.\n- The generalised test is proven RED by real removal, not asserted.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`.\n\n## Notes\n\nFound by the Gerrit `LLM-Review` bot on change 2256 patch set 7 (advisory, non-blocking,\nso it did not stop the merge). Verified present on `origin/main` after 2256 landed.\nDiscovered from `e668-b496-e264-4283`.","id":"724c-b5fd-efb1-40d5","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"mutmut run in _diagnose_non_killed executes mutants unsandboxed"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787855575448865001,"uuid":"2eb5e7a6-c9ca-4e66-8388-a72f767dee02"}